Let $C$ be the set of students taking calculus, $P$ be the set of students taking physics, and $Ch$ be the set of students taking chemistry.
We are given the following information:
Total number of students = 360
$|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$
$|C \cap P \cap Ch^c| = 15$
$|C| = 180$
$|Ch| = 2|P|$
$|C \cap Ch| = 75$
$|P \cap Ch| = 75$
$|C \cap P| = 30$
We want to find $|P|$.

We know that $|C \cup P \cup Ch| \le 360$.
We have the inclusion-exclusion principle:
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = |C| + |P| + |Ch| - |C \cap P| - |C \cap Ch| - |P \cap Ch| + |C \cap P \cap Ch|$
Plugging in the given values, we have:
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 180 + |P| + 2|P| - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 180 + 3|P| - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 180 - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15 + 3|P|$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 180 - 180 + 15 + 3|P|$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 15 + 3|P|$

We are also given that 15 students take calculus, physics, and chemistry. Thus $|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$.
We are also given that 15 students don't take any of the three subjects.
Thus the number of students who take at least one subject is $360 - 15 = 345$.
So $|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 345$.
Therefore, $15 + 3|P| = 345$.
$3|P| = 345 - 15$
$3|P| = 330$
$|P| = \frac{330}{3}$
$|P| = 110$

Now we check this result.
$|C| = 180$, $|P| = 110$, $|Ch| = 2|P| = 220$
$|C \cap P| = 30$, $|C \cap Ch| = 75$, $|P \cap Ch| = 75$
$|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 180 + 110 + 220 - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15$
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 510 - 180 + 15 = 330 + 15 = 345$
The number of students who take at least one subject is 345, which is $360 - 15$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{110}$
