Let $f(x) = x^2 + 3x$ and $g(x) = x - 1$.
We want to determine if $f(x)g(x)$ is even, odd, or neither.
First, we find $f(x)g(x)$:
$$f(x)g(x) = (x^2 + 3x)(x - 1) = x^3 - x^2 + 3x^2 - 3x = x^3 + 2x^2 - 3x$$
Now, we check if $f(x)g(x)$ is an even function. A function $h(x)$ is even if $h(-x) = h(x)$ for all $x$.
$$f(x)g(-x) = (-x)^3 + 2(-x)^2 - 3(-x) = -x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x$$
Since $f(x)g(-x) \neq f(x)g(x)$, $f(x)g(x)$ is not an even function.
Next, we check if $f(x)g(x)$ is an odd function. A function $h(x)$ is odd if $h(-x) = -h(x)$ for all $x$.
$$-f(x)g(-x) = -(x^3 + 2x^2 - 3x) = -x^3 - 2x^2 + 3x$$
Since $-f(x)g(-x) \neq f(x)g(x)$, $f(x)g(x)$ is not an odd function.
Therefore, $f(x)g(x)$ is neither even nor odd.

