We know that a number is divisible by $9$ if and only if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9$.
Since the number is $aaa$, the sum of its digits is $a+a+a = 3a$.
We want $3a$ to be divisible by $9$, so $3a \equiv 0 \pmod{9}$. Dividing by $3$ gives $a \equiv 0 \pmod{3}$, which means $a$ must be a multiple of $3$. Since we are looking for the smallest 3-digit number, we take the smallest possible value for $a$ such that $a$ is a digit (i.e., $a$ is an integer between 0 and 9).
So $a = 0, 3, 6, 9$. We want the smallest 3-digit number, so we take $a = 3$.
Then the number is $333$, and $333 = 111 \times 3$.
Since $333 = 3 \cdot 111 = 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 37 = 9 \cdot 37$, $333$ is divisible by $9$.

The smallest 3-digit number of the form $aaa$ which is divisible by $9$ is $333$.

