We will use synthetic division to divide $x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 + 11x - 9$ by $x^2 - 3x + 2$. 
First, we set up the synthetic division. Since we are dividing by $x^2 - 3x + 2$, we use the coefficients of the polynomial $x^2 - 3x + 2$, which are 1, -3, and 2.

\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
  \multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & +0x & +1 \\
  \cline{2-6}
  x^2-3x+2 & x^4 & -3x^3 & +4x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
  \multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & -3x^3 & +2x^2 \\
  \cline{2-4}
  \multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 2x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
  \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2x^2 & -6x & +4 \\
  \cline{4-6}
  \multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 17x & -13 \\
\end{array}

The quotient is $x^2+1$ and the remainder is $17x - 13$.

The division is:
$$ \frac{x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 + 11x - 9}{x^2 - 3x + 2} = x^2 + 1 + \frac{17x - 13}{x^2 - 3x + 2} $$
The remainder is $17x - 13$.

